Let $G$ be an infinite connected locally finite quasi-transitive graph with $h_V(G)>0$. For Bernoulli bond percolation on $G$, $$\sup_{0\le p<{p_c}}\lVert T_p\rVert_{2\to2}
       =\lVert T_{{p_c}}\rVert_{2\to2}<\infty,
 \qquad {p_c}(G)<{p_{2\to 2}}(G)\le{p_u}(G).$$ There exist ${p_c}<p_1<p_2<1$ such that, in the coupling obtained from independent uniform edge labels, almost surely there are infinitely many infinite open clusters simultaneously for every $p\in[p_1,p_2]$.

Let $G$ be an infinite connected locally finite quasi-transitive graph with $h_V(G)>0$. For Bernoulli bond percolation on $G$, $$\label{eq:critical-triangle}
 \nabla_{{p_c}}(v)\le\lVert T_{{p_c}}\rVert_{2\to2}^{3}<\infty
 \qquad\text{for every }v\in V.$$ For every $v\in V$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \chi_v(p)&\asymp({p_c}-p)^{-1} &&(p\uparrow{p_c}), \label{eq:meanfield-chi}\\
 \theta_v(p)&\asymp p-{p_c}&&(p\downarrow{p_c}), \label{eq:meanfield-theta}\\
 \mathbb P_{{p_c}}(|C_v|\ge n)&\asymp n^{-1/2} &&(n\to\infty), \label{eq:meanfield-size}\\
 \mathbb P_{{p_c}}\bigl(\operatorname{rad}_{\mathrm{int}}(C_v)\ge n\bigr)
    &\asymp n^{-1} &&(n\to\infty), \label{eq:meanfield-intrinsic}\\
 \mathbb P_{{p_c}}\bigl(\operatorname{rad}_{\mathrm{ext}}(C_v)\ge n\bigr)
    &\asymp n^{-1} &&(n\to\infty). \label{eq:meanfield-extrinsic}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $n$ tends through positive integers. The comparison constants and the one-sided neighborhoods of ${p_c}$ may depend on $G$ and $v$. Moreover, $$\label{eq:exponential-threshold}
 {p_c}(G)<{p_{2\to 2}}(G)\le{p_{\exp}}(G).$$ For each fixed $p\in[0,{p_{2\to 2}}(G))$, there are constants $0<A_{G,p}<\infty$ and $a_{G,p}>0$ such that $$\label{eq:exponential-connectivity}
 T_p(x,y)\le A_{G,p}e^{-a_{G,p}\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}_G(x,y)}
 \qquad\text{for every }x,y\in V.$$

Increasing nonnegative functions of independent percolation bits have nonnegative covariance. If increasing events have disjoint finite open witnesses, their joint disjoint-occurrence probability is at most the product of their probabilities. In particular, for all $x,y,z$, $$\label{eq:s01}
 \mathbb P_p(x\leftrightarrow y,\ x\leftrightarrow z)
 \le\sum_w T_p(x,w)T_p(w,y)T_p(w,z).$$ The same assertions hold in every induced domain.

For every invariant nonnegative function $m$ on ordered vertex pairs, $$\label{eq:s02}
 \left[\sum_y m(o,y)\right]
 =\left[\sum_x m(x,o)\right].$$ The identity also applies to the expectation of a covariant random mass and to absolutely summable signed or complex masses. In particular, an invariant stochastic kernel on a single orbit has column sums one, whether or not it is symmetric.

The kernel $P$ is positive semidefinite and $$\label{eq:s03}
 \rho:=\lVert P\rVert<1.$$ Its projection onto the vertex types is an irreducible lazy finite Markov chain. There exist $c_*>0$ and $l_0<\infty$ such that $$\sum_{y\in O_j}P^l(x,y)\ge c_*
 \quad\text{for every }x\in V,\ j\le h_0,\ l\ge l_0.$$

The map $p\mapsto T_p$ is locally absolutely continuous in operator norm on $(0,{p_c})$, and $\chi$ is locally absolutely continuous there. For almost every $p\in(0,{p_c})$, $$\label{eq:s04}
 \begin{aligned}
 (1-p)T'_p(x,y)
 &=\sum_a\sum_{b\sim a}
   \mathbb P_p(x\in C_a,\ y\in C_b,\ C_a\ne C_b),\\
 (1-p)\chi'
 &=\left[\sum_{b\sim o}
   \mathbb E_p(s_o s_b;\ C_o\ne C_b)\right].
 \end{aligned}$$

For $0\le p,a\le1$, $$\label{eq:s05}
 T_{p+(1-p)a}
 \le\sum_{j\ge0}a^jT_p(\mathop{\mathrm{Adj}}T_p)^j
 \qquad\text{entrywise}.$$ If $T_p$ is bounded and $ad\lVert T_p\rVert<1$, the kernel on the left is bounded. In particular, boundedness of $T_{p_c}$ implies ${p_c}<{p_{2\to 2}}$.

For each $\epsilon\in(0,1/2)$, uniformly in $x$ and for all sufficiently large $M$, $$\label{eq:s06}
 \mathbb P_{p_c}(s_x\le M,\ C_x\text{ is not good for cap }M)
 \le e^{-M^\epsilon}.$$ The event that a cluster has size at most $M$ and is good for cap $M$ is determined by the percolation bits in a deterministic finite-radius ball about its root.

Fix the output fields, $x,z$, the old-map seeds, and the priorities ordering contact lists. A valid switching input can be specified by the selected indices and positive gaps locating the selected seams in the ordered list of bridges separating $z$ from $x$, read from $z$ toward $x$. Each fixed code determines at most one input bit tuple, without using the fresh contact uniforms. After integrating those uniforms, the total mass of valid codes is at most $$\label{eq:s12}
 W^k,\qquad
 W=1+\sum_{l=1}^{M}\min\{1,2M^s/l\}
       \le C M^s\log M.$$

With $F(x,z)=\mathbb EL_x(z,D')$, one has $$\label{eq:s13}
 \begin{split}
 F(x,z)&=\frac{u^k}{n}\sum_{y\in O}
  \mathbb P_{{p_c}}(y\in C_x,\ n\le s_x<2n)K^k(z,y),\\
 c\pi u^k&\le b_0\le C\pi u^k,
 \qquad \lVert F/b_0\rVert\le r^k.
 \end{split}$$

There is a fixed polynomial $Q(k,M)\ge1$ such that $$\label{eq:s14}
 \mathbb EL^2\le Q(k,M)\pi u^{2k}
 \qquad\text{whenever } kM\le n/2
               \text{ and } n\ge(W/u)^k.$$

Fix an old map. For $n\in\mathcal N$ tending to infinity and $k=O(\log n)$ with $kM\le n/2$ and $n\ge(W/u)^k$, there is, for all sufficiently large such $n$, a new local map with parameters satisfying $$\label{eq:s15}
 u'\ge c'\pi/Q(k,M),\qquad r'\le2r^k,
 \qquad M'=2n+kM.$$ The threshold for $n$ may depend on the fixed old map.

There is a fixed $C>0$ such that, with $c_k=e^{Ck}$, $$\label{def:edge-weight}
 \sum_{w:\,e\in E(\gamma_w)}c(w)\le c_k/k
 \quad\text{for every edge }e.$$ On a connection in a finite cluster, $N^D_{xy}\le c_k|C_x^D|$. For fixed $t$, $W_t^D(x,y)$ is increasing under edge openings and under domain enlargement, using restriction of the same configuration. The kernel $T_t$ is invariant, symmetric, and entrywise between zero and $T_0$, and $$\label{def:row-norm}
 m_t\le\sup_x s_t^V(x)\le c_\sigma\chi_t.$$ The functions $T_t(x,y)$ and $s_t^D(x)$ are continuously differentiable for $t\ge0$, with a right derivative at zero, by differentiating $W_t^D(x,y)$ to $-N^D_{xy}W_t^D(x,y)$ under expectation and summation.

For every $t\ge0$, $$\label{eq:s27}
 0\le T_0-T_t\le 2t\,T_0P^kT_0\quad\text{entrywise},\qquad
 m_t\ge m_0-2t\rho^km_0^2.$$

For $A\subset D$, let $\mathop{\mathrm{Nb}}[A]$ denote $A$ together with all its neighbors in the full graph. For every vertex $v$, $$\label{eq:s28}
 s_t^{D\setminus A}(v)
 \ge s_t^D(v)-\chi_0^+\sum_{z\in\mathop{\mathrm{Nb}}[A]}T_0(v,z).$$

There is a constant $C'>0$ such that, for every induced domain $D$ and $u,v\in D$, $$\label{eq:s29}
 \mathbb E_p\bigl(X_t^D(u)X_t^D(v);\ C_u^D\ne C_v^D\bigr)
 \ge s_t^D(u)s_t^D(v)-C'(\chi_0^+)^2T_0^3(u,v).$$ The constant is independent of $D,p,k,t$.
